-- chem/h2-net.hypercubical — two protons and two electrons on the smallest cage of the diamond net (ten vertices),
-- the electrostatic part alone. The cage is closed and the four point charges sum to zero, so no wall is needed
-- (chem/charge.hypercubical's `field-energy`, in units of one line's own energy). With the electrons on the two protons
-- the net is neutral at every vertex and the flux and its energy are zero, at any separation of the protons
-- (`h2-neutral`, protons one line apart and three lines apart). Moving one electron off its proton to the bridge
-- vertex between two three-line vertices costs 3/4, and moving both to one bridge costs 2 (`h2-charged`). So with the
-- hop off the lowest state is the two neutral atoms and there is no electrostatic binding: the molecule's binding is
-- the hop's, as in chem/h2plus-net.hypercubical (one electron) and chem/netatom.hypercubical. The two-electron bound
-- state is the hop on the cover of chem/cover.hypercubical over the two electrons' positions with this potential and the
-- spin-singlet share of chem/charge.hypercubical's `shared-vertex-allows`; a statement here, not yet a program.
-- A leaf: no module imports it.
import "fluxsplit.hypercubical"

